$body = <<<'HTML'
<div class="takeaways">
  <p class="takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Balanced attic ventilation, with cool air entering at the soffits and hot air leaving near the ridge, lowers attic temperatures, protects shingles and controls moisture.</li>
    <li>The common sizing rule is 1 sq ft of net free vent area per 150 sq ft of attic floor, or 1 per 300 when intake and exhaust are balanced. Aim for at least as much intake as exhaust.</li>
    <li>In Austin, many attics also hold ductwork and the AC air handler, so a cooler attic directly reduces cooling losses.</li>
    <li>Don't mix exhaust types such as ridge vents and power fans, and never vent bathroom fans into the attic.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<p class="lead">Attic ventilation upgrade benefits go well beyond a cooler attic. A properly balanced system lowers the heat load on your AC, extends shingle life, keeps insulation dry and effective, and protects the roof deck and framing from moisture. For homes in Austin and across Central Texas, where summer attics routinely exceed 140°F, better ventilation is one of the most cost-effective roof upgrades available, especially when it's done during a roof replacement.</p>

<p>This guide explains how attic ventilation works, how to size intake and exhaust, and what each benefit means for a Texas home. Austin Roofing evaluates ventilation on every inspection, because it affects shingle warranties and roof lifespan as much as the shingles themselves.</p>

<h2 id="h-how-proper-attic-ventilation-systems-manage-heat-and-moisture-movement">How Attic Ventilation Works: Intake, Exhaust and Airflow</h2>
<p>A vented attic works by natural convection and wind pressure. Cooler outside air enters low, through soffit or eave vents. It warms as it moves up beneath the roof deck and exits high, through ridge vents, box vents or gable vents. That steady flow carries away the heat and water vapor that build up in the attic.</p>
<div class="feature-grid">
  <div class="feature"><h3>Intake</h3><p>Continuous or individual soffit vents, or edge vents at the eave on homes without soffits.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Exhaust</h3><p>Ridge vent is usually the most effective. Static box vents, turbines or gable vents are alternatives.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Clear pathway</h3><p>Baffles (rafter vents) keep blown insulation from blocking airflow at the eaves.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Balance</h3><p>Exhaust can only pull out as much air as the intake lets in.</p></div>
</div>

<h3>How to size intake and exhaust: the 1/150 and 1/300 rules</h3>
<p>Ventilation is measured in <strong>net free area (NFA)</strong>, the actual open area of a vent after screens and louvers. Model building codes (IRC R806) set a minimum of <strong>1 sq ft of NFA per 150 sq ft</strong> of attic floor. That can drop to <strong>1 per 300</strong> when the system is balanced, with roughly 40–50% of the vent area in the upper part of the attic as exhaust and the rest as intake. Shingle manufacturers commonly reference the same ratios. Check the code edition your city has adopted.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Attic floor area</th><th>Total NFA at 1/300</th><th>Intake (≈50–60%)</th><th>Exhaust (≈40–50%)</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>1,200 sq ft</td><td>4 sq ft (576 sq in)</td><td>≈288–346 sq in</td><td>≈230–288 sq in</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1,500 sq ft</td><td>5 sq ft (720 sq in)</td><td>≈360–432 sq in</td><td>≈288–360 sq in</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2,000 sq ft</td><td>6.67 sq ft (960 sq in)</td><td>≈480–576 sq in</td><td>≈384–480 sq in</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2,500 sq ft</td><td>8.33 sq ft (1,200 sq in)</td><td>≈600–720 sq in</td><td>≈480–600 sq in</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> a 1,500 sq ft attic at 1/300 needs 720 sq in of NFA. Split it so intake is at least half, say 380 sq in intake and 340 sq in exhaust. Many ridge vent products are rated somewhere around 12–18 sq in of NFA per linear foot (check the product label), so 340 sq in calls for roughly 19–28 feet of ridge vent. If the ridge is too short, add static vents near the ridge rather than a different exhaust type. At the 1/150 rule the totals simply double.</p>
<div class="callout callout--warn"><p><strong>Don't short-circuit the system.</strong> Mixing a ridge vent with power fans, turbines or open gable vents lets one exhaust pull air in through another instead of through the soffits, leaving the lower attic stagnant. Power fans can also pull conditioned air out of the house through ceiling gaps.</p></div>

<h2 id="h-energy-efficiency-improvements-that-reduce-cooling-costs-and-hvac-workload">Energy Efficiency: Lower Cooling Costs in Texas Heat</h2>
<p>On a July afternoon in Austin, the sun heats the roof deck and that heat radiates down into the attic. Without good airflow, attic air can approach 150°F or more. In many Central Texas homes the ductwork and air handler sit in that attic, so the AC has to fight the attic as well as the outdoors. Every leaky duct joint loses cooled air into an oven.</p>
<p>Ventilation won't make an attic as cool as the outdoors, but bringing peak temperatures down meaningfully reduces heat gain through the ceiling and ducts. Pair ventilation with good insulation and air sealing, and consider a radiant barrier, and you attack the problem from every side. Results vary by home, so be wary of anyone promising a specific percentage off your bill.</p>

<h2 id="h-extending-roof-lifespan-by-reducing-heat-stress-and-seasonal-damage">Extending Roof Lifespan by Reducing Heat Stress</h2>
<p>Asphalt shingles age through heat and UV, which drive off the oils that keep them flexible. A superheated attic bakes shingles from below while the sun bakes them from above. Over time that speeds up granule loss, curling and cracking, which are the signs we see on under-ventilated Austin roofs that look older than they are. Most shingle manufacturers require adequate ventilation as a warranty condition. Our <a href="/blog/residential-roofing-warranty-coverage-guide-for-homeowners/">roofing warranty guide</a> explains why that matters, and the <a href="/roofing-tools/roof-lifespan-calculator/">roof lifespan calculator</a> shows how conditions affect expected life.</p>

<h2 id="h-moisture-control-strategies-that-help-prevent-mold-rot-and-insulation-damage">Moisture Control: Preventing Mold, Rot and Insulation Damage</h2>
<p>Central Texas isn't a cold, snowy climate, but it is humid. Moisture reaches attics from humid outside air, from showers and cooking, and from bath or kitchen fans that dump air into the attic instead of outdoors. On cool winter nights the underside of the roof deck can drop below the dew point, and condensation forms. Ventilation flushes that moisture before it can:</p>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>Grow mold on the underside of the decking</li>
  <li>Delaminate OSB or plywood and rust roofing nails</li>
  <li>Compress and soak insulation, reducing its R-value</li>
  <li>Stain ceilings and create musty odors</li>
</ul>
<p>Always route bath and kitchen exhaust fans through the roof or a wall with a proper vent cap. Never let them end in the attic.</p>

<h2 id="h-improving-whole-home-comfort-by-stabilizing-upper-floor-temperatures">Whole-Home Comfort: More Even Upstairs Temperatures</h2>
<p>If the second floor of your Round Rock or Cedar Park home is several degrees warmer than downstairs in summer, a hot attic is often part of the reason. Less heat radiating through the ceiling means a more even house and fewer thermostat battles, though duct design and insulation play a role too.</p>

<h2 id="h-enhancing-insulation-effectiveness-through-better-airflow-management">Better Insulation Performance</h2>
<p>Insulation slows heat flow, but it works best when it is dry, evenly distributed and not packed into the eaves. Ventilation upgrades should include rafter baffles at each bay so blown-in insulation can be topped up without blocking soffit intake. Many older Austin homes have soffit vents that were buried under insulation decades ago and do almost nothing.</p>

<h2 id="h-reducing-wear-and-tear-on-heating-and-cooling-equipment">Less Wear on Heating and Cooling Equipment</h2>
<p>An air handler and ducts sitting in a cooler attic lose less capacity, so the system runs shorter cycles to reach the setpoint. Over a long Texas cooling season that can reduce strain on compressors and blowers. Ventilation won't fix an undersized or failing AC system, but it removes some of the heat load working against it.</p>

<h2 id="h-protecting-structural-components-from-long-term-heat-and-moisture-exposure">Protecting the Roof Deck and Framing</h2>
<p>Repeated heat and moisture cycles stress the roof deck, rafters and trusses. Constant moisture leads to soft decking and rot, which we often find during tear-offs around poorly vented areas and below bath fans that vent into the attic. Replacing decking adds cost to any <a href="/services/roof-replacement/">roof replacement</a>, so preventing it pays off.</p>

<h2 id="h-supporting-healthier-indoor-air-quality-throughout-the-home">Healthier Indoor Air Quality</h2>
<p>Mold and musty air in the attic can find their way into the living space through ceiling penetrations, can lights and leaky return ducts. Keeping the attic dry, and sealing the ceiling plane so attic air stays in the attic, supports cleaner indoor air.</p>

<h2 id="h-increasing-home-value-through-energy-efficiency-and-building-durability">Increasing Home Value and Durability</h2>
<p>Home inspectors in the Austin market check attics for ventilation, insulation and moisture stains. A well-vented attic with a clean deck, dry insulation and correctly terminated exhaust fans is one less issue in a buyer's report. Combined with a newer roof, it's a selling point.</p>

<h2>Common Attic Ventilation Upgrades and What They Involve</h2>
<p>Most ventilation upgrades fall into a handful of tasks. The right mix depends on how your roof is framed and what is already there:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th>Upgrade</th><th>What it fixes</th><th>Best timing</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Continuous ridge vent</td><td>Replaces scattered box vents with even exhaust along the peak</td><td>During re-roof (ridge is cut open)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Additional soffit intake</td><td>Starved intake, the most common problem we find</td><td>Any time; done from below</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Rafter baffles</td><td>Insulation blocking soffit vents</td><td>Any time; ideal before adding insulation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Edge or drip-edge intake vents</td><td>Homes with little or no soffit</td><td>During re-roof</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Removing redundant exhaust</td><td>Short-circuiting between gable vents, turbines and ridge vent</td><td>During re-roof</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Rerouting bath and kitchen fans</td><td>Moisture dumped into the attic</td><td>Any time</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>A good assessment starts in the attic, not on the roof. We look at the soffits from inside to see whether daylight comes through, check for stained or frosted decking, measure the attic floor area and ridge length, and note where ducts, the air handler and any exhaust fans sit. That information shows whether the fix is as simple as clearing insulation from the eaves or calls for a rebalanced system.</p>

<h3>Signs your attic needs a ventilation upgrade</h3>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>The attic feels unbearably hot in late morning, not just at peak afternoon</li>
  <li>Dark staining, mildew or rusty nail tips on the underside of the decking</li>
  <li>Shingles curling or shedding granules years earlier than expected</li>
  <li>Upstairs rooms that never cool down despite a properly sized AC</li>
  <li>Only one or two box vents for the whole roof, or no visible soffit vents</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout callout--tip"><p><strong>Spray-foam (unvented) attics are different.</strong> Some Central Texas homes have closed-cell or open-cell foam sprayed on the underside of the roof deck, which creates a conditioned attic. Those attics should <em>not</em> have soffit or ridge vents added. Mixing the two approaches can cause condensation problems. Have your attic assessed before changing anything.</p></div>

<h2 id="h-a-smart-long-term-upgrade-that-strengthens-the-home-from-the-top-down">A Smart Upgrade, and When to Do It</h2>
<p>The best time to upgrade attic ventilation is during a roof replacement. The ridge can be cut open for continuous ridge vent, old box vents removed and patched, and intake added, all for a small fraction of the cost of the whole project. Between replacements, Austin Roofing can still add or correct vents, clear blocked soffits and replace failed turbines. Start with a free <a href="/services/roof-inspection/">roof and attic inspection</a>, or read <a href="/blog/how-much-does-it-cost-to-replace-a-roof/">what a roof replacement costs</a> if you are planning ahead. We serve homeowners across Austin, <a href="/service-areas/pflugerville-tx/">Pflugerville</a>, <a href="/service-areas/kyle-tx/">Kyle</a> and the rest of Central Texas. <a href="/contact-us/">Contact us</a> to schedule.</p>
HTML;

